From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Diljit Singh <hey@djdev.me>
Date: Sat, 7 Nov 2026 07:40:23 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] usb: dwc3: apple: keep the host through a deep sleep

macOS keeps its host through a sleep: the hub isn't reset, xHCI's state
comes back in place and the devices keep their slots. For a host behind
a fixed hub whose PHY can take the core through hibernation, don't stop
it for a deep sleep. xHCI suspends and resumes as on any platform,
saving and restoring its state, and the PHY hibernates the core in
between, see apple_atcphy_sleep_prepare(). The wakeup setting stays: the
glue is a syscore device while its core runs, so genpd keeps away from
its domain either way, and the sleep's commit does the lowering.

- macOS's T8140 controller reset sets GCTL's hibernation enable and a
  power-down scale of 0xd. Without the enable the core accepts a D3
  request but saves and restores nothing. Set both after every core init
  on T8140, since the soft reset undoes them.
- After the core's D0 macOS polls the PIPE PHY's ready bit, bit 30 of
  +0x2c in the DRD config block, for 100 ms, and only logs a timeout.
  Map that block and do the same ahead of xHCI's restore.
- The GUID scratch register is marked before the sleep and read back
  after. A glue hook in xHCI's resume quirk checks it and the PHY's
  result before xHCI's first access. A core in D0 that lost its
  registers is set up again in place, the way the bring-up does after
  its reset's release: dwc3_core_init(), dropping the PHY references it
  takes a second time, the CIO setup, the tunables, the hibernation
  setup and the host role, then the USB3 PHY's host mode as the role
  switch sets it, which routes the PIPE to a PHY that came back in USB3
  before xHCI starts. The hook then tells xHCI the controller lost
  its state, and xHCI starts it over: the root hubs and their devices
  reset-resume and keep their drivers and input nodes, and the next
  sleep hibernates again. If that setup fails the host is restarted as
  after a stop, and the devices enumerate again. A core that may be
  unpowered is left alone: USB stays off until a reboot and later
  sleeps are refused. A sleep refused before the hibernation left the
  core as it was, and xHCI resumes it as usual.
- A fixed-hub host started after a sleep, by the stop path or that
  restart, starts with no role, as at probe, so dwc3_core_soft_reset()
  runs as well: on a J700 the hub didn't come back after a restart
  without it, and did when the driver was bound again. After every
  sleep of a fixed-hub host the glue checks 5 s later that the hub is on
  its root port, the hub node's reg. If it isn't, the host is stopped,
  the hub's link left down for half a second and the host started
  again, once.
- The PHY's SuperSpeed check after a wake, and any mux update it needs,
  runs from a work item rather than from the PM notifier, so the resume
  doesn't wait for it; the next sleep's PM_SUSPEND_PREPARE waits for it
  to end.
- Before the freeze, a host that sleeps in place records which of the
  fixed hub's ports have a device at or below them with wakeup enabled,
  the setting usbcore goes by when it arms remote wakeup for a system
  sleep, for the port controllers' USB wake. The hub's own setting
  doesn't count, as it's on the board. The record is cleared after the
  sleep, and stays clear for a host that stops. A setting or a device
  that changes between the notifier and the freeze isn't seen.

Everywhere else, and for a PHY that can't hibernate the core, the glue
stops the controller as before.

Measured on a J700: the core loses its registers in every sleep so far,
and after each wake the hub and a USB keyboard on it reset-resume
instead of enumerating again, and the SuperSpeed side is in its mode
again when the glue checks it.

Signed-off-by: Diljit Singh <hey@djdev.me>

[j604 backport: this tree's glue maps no DRD config block (no drd_cfg) and applies no tunables, so dwc3_apple_core_reinit() drops the tunables call and dwc3_apple_pre_host_resume() drops the 100 ms PIPE PHY ready poll of +0x2c (both only reached by a host that sleeps in place, which needs a T8140 PHY); <linux/iopoll.h> not added (unused then), no host_only field. "io.h" is included for dwc3_readl()/dwc3_writel() (Aurora's glue had it already; its tracepoints are exported by the c506653127 pick before this). Everything else as in the original. On J604 (no fixed hub, T8132 PHY) the glue keeps stopping the controllers for a deep sleep; pre_host_resume returns 0 there.]
Signed-off-by: Dylan Ironside <dylan@dylanironside.io>
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5.5
---
 drivers/usb/dwc3/core.h       |   3 +
 drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-apple.c | 421 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++-
 drivers/usb/dwc3/host.c       |  12 +
 3 files changed, 424 insertions(+), 12 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/usb/dwc3/core.h b/drivers/usb/dwc3/core.h
index 150af8cad6..6ccc6830a7 100644
--- a/drivers/usb/dwc3/core.h
+++ b/drivers/usb/dwc3/core.h
@@ -1003,10 +1003,13 @@ struct dwc3_scratchpad_array {
  *				need to be passed on to glue layer
  * @pre_set_role: Notify glue of role switch notifications
  * @pre_run_stop: Notify run stop enable/disable information to glue
+ * @pre_host_resume: Before xHCI touches the core at a system resume; an error keeps it away,
+ *		     and a positive value says the core lost its state and was set up again
  */
 struct dwc3_glue_ops {
 	void	(*pre_set_role)(struct dwc3 *dwc, enum usb_role role);
 	void	(*pre_run_stop)(struct dwc3 *dwc, bool is_on);
+	int	(*pre_host_resume)(struct dwc3 *dwc);
 };
 
 /**
diff --git a/drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-apple.c b/drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-apple.c
index c7e3112ee5..1cbffc6d02 100644
--- a/drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-apple.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/dwc3/dwc3-apple.c
@@ -8,19 +8,26 @@
  *  - dwc3-of-simple.c Copyright (c) 2015 Texas Instruments Incorporated - https://www.ti.com
  */
 
+#include <linux/delay.h>
+#include <linux/devm-helpers.h>
 #include <linux/of.h>
 #include <linux/module.h>
 #include <linux/mutex.h>
+#include <linux/phy/apple-atc.h>
 #include <linux/phy/phy.h>
 #include <linux/platform_device.h>
 #include <linux/pm_runtime.h>
 #include <linux/pm_wakeup.h>
 #include <linux/reset.h>
+#include <linux/soc/apple/sleep.h>
 #include <linux/suspend.h>
+#include <linux/usb.h>
+#include <linux/usb/hcd.h>
 #include <linux/usb/phy.h>
 
 #include "dwc3-apple-state.h"
 #include "glue.h"
+#include "io.h"
 
 /*
  * This platform requires a very specific sequence of operations to bring up dwc3 and its USB3 PHY:
@@ -88,9 +95,13 @@
  * dwc3_apple_init(). In host mode xHCI then keeps running in suspend-to-idle when a USB device
  * may wake the system, as a halted xHC could not signal the wakeup.
  *
- * A deep sleep takes the controller's power domain away, so nothing can be kept running across
- * it. The glue stops everything before the freezer runs, the way a cable disconnect does, and
- * brings it back once the system has resumed. See dwc3_apple_pm_notify().
+ * A deep sleep takes the controller's power domain away. Behind a fixed hub on T8140 the host
+ * sleeps in place, as macOS's does: xHCI suspends and resumes as usual, and the PHY takes the
+ * core through hibernation in between, so the hub and its devices stay. A core that comes
+ * back without its registers is set up again in place and xHCI starts over, so the devices
+ * reset-resume. Anywhere else, or when the PHY can't, the glue stops everything before the
+ * freezer runs, the way a cable disconnect does, and brings it back once the system has
+ * resumed. See dwc3_apple_pm_notify().
  *
  * Runtime PM (T6040 and T8132, see dwc3_apple_core_probe()): the glue holds a reference from
  * the reset deassert to the end of the shutdown, so with no cable the controller can
@@ -138,6 +149,8 @@ struct dwc3_apple {
 	struct phy *usb3_phy;
 	struct usb_role_switch *role_sw;
 	bool fixed_hub;
+	/* The root port the fixed hub sits on, its node's reg. */
+	u32 hub_port;
 
 	struct mutex lock;
 
@@ -153,6 +166,17 @@ struct dwc3_apple {
 	bool pre_core_rpm;
 	bool dt_dis_u3_susphy;
 	struct notifier_block pm_nb;
+	/* Kept through a deep sleep, with the PHY's hibernation, and the GUID it had. */
+	bool sleep_in_place;
+	u32 sleep_guid;
+	/* The core lost its registers in that sleep and was set up again in place. */
+	bool sleep_reinit;
+	/* Looks for the fixed hub once a host restarted after a sleep has had time to find it. */
+	struct delayed_work hub_check;
+	struct work_struct sleep_finish;
+	bool sleep_finish_kept;
+	/* The core didn't come back from a sleep, and nothing may touch it until a reboot. */
+	bool sleep_dead;
 	/* Stopped for a deep sleep: the state to come back to, and the wakeup setting. */
 	bool sleep_stopped;
 	bool sleep_wakeup;
@@ -205,6 +229,27 @@ static inline void dwc3_apple_mask(struct dwc3_apple *appledwc, u32 offset, u32
 	dwc3_apple_writel(appledwc, offset, reg);
 }
 
+/*
+ * macOS's T8140 controller reset sets GCTL's hibernation enable and a power-down scale
+ * of 0xd. Without the enable the core's power management accepts a
+ * D0/D3 request but saves and restores nothing, so the PHY's hibernation for a sleep
+ * needs it.
+ */
+#define DWC3_APPLE_T8140_PWRDNSCALE 0xd
+
+static void dwc3_apple_setup_hibernation(struct dwc3_apple *appledwc)
+{
+	struct dwc3 *dwc = &appledwc->dwc;
+	u32 reg;
+
+	if (!of_device_is_compatible(appledwc->dev->of_node, "apple,t8140-dwc3"))
+		return;
+	reg = dwc3_readl(dwc, DWC3_GCTL);
+	reg &= ~DWC3_GCTL_PWRDNSCALE_MASK;
+	reg |= DWC3_GCTL_PWRDNSCALE(DWC3_APPLE_T8140_PWRDNSCALE) | DWC3_GCTL_GBLHIBERNATIONEN;
+	dwc3_writel(dwc, DWC3_GCTL, reg);
+}
+
 static void dwc3_apple_setup_cio(struct dwc3_apple *appledwc)
 {
 	dwc3_apple_writel(appledwc, APPLE_DWC3_CIO_LFPS_OFFSET, APPLE_DWC3_CIO_LFPS_OFFSET_VALUE);
@@ -226,6 +271,84 @@ static void dwc3_apple_set_ptrcap(struct dwc3_apple *appledwc, u32 mode)
 	dwc3_set_prtcap(&appledwc->dwc, mode, false);
 }
 
+/* Written to the core's GUID scratch register before a sleep in place, read back after it. */
+#define DWC3_APPLE_SLEEP_GUID 0x534c5045
+
+/*
+ * The core is powered but its registers are at their reset values. Set it up again the way
+ * dwc3_apple_core_start() and dwc3_apple_set_role() do, in place: the PHYs and the xHCI
+ * device stay, so xHCI only has to start over and its devices reset-resume. Runs in the
+ * resume, while nothing else touches the core.
+ */
+static int dwc3_apple_core_reinit(struct dwc3_apple *appledwc)
+{
+	struct dwc3 *dwc = &appledwc->dwc;
+	int i, ret;
+
+	ret = dwc3_core_init(dwc);
+	if (ret)
+		return ret;
+	/* dwc3_core_init() took PHY references the core holds already. */
+	for (i = 0; i < dwc->num_usb3_ports; i++) {
+		phy_power_off(dwc->usb3_generic_phy[i]);
+		phy_exit(dwc->usb3_generic_phy[i]);
+	}
+	for (i = 0; i < dwc->num_usb2_ports; i++) {
+		phy_power_off(dwc->usb2_generic_phy[i]);
+		phy_exit(dwc->usb2_generic_phy[i]);
+	}
+
+	dwc3_apple_setup_cio(appledwc);
+	dwc3_apple_setup_hibernation(appledwc);
+	dwc3_apple_set_ptrcap(appledwc, DWC3_GCTL_PRTCAP_HOST);
+	dwc->dis_u3_susphy_quirk = appledwc->dt_dis_u3_susphy;
+	dwc3_enable_susphy(dwc, true);
+	/* A PHY that came back in a USB3 mode routes the PIPE to it now, before xHCI starts. */
+	ret = phy_set_mode(appledwc->usb3_phy, PHY_MODE_USB_HOST);
+	if (ret)
+		dev_warn(appledwc->dev, "USB3 PHY setup failed after the sleep, USB2 only, err=%d\n",
+			 ret);
+
+	return 0;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Runs before xHCI touches the core after a sleep in place. A core that may be unpowered must
+ * be left alone, as an access could be an SError: dwc3_apple_sleep_kept() deals with it once
+ * the system is back. A core that came back without its registers is set up again, and xHCI
+ * is told it lost its state (a positive return). A sleep refused before the hibernation left
+ * the core as it was.
+ */
+static int dwc3_apple_pre_host_resume(struct dwc3 *dwc)
+{
+	struct dwc3_apple *appledwc = to_dwc3_apple(dwc);
+	int ret;
+
+	if (!READ_ONCE(appledwc->sleep_in_place))
+		return 0;
+
+	ret = apple_atcphy_sleep_result(appledwc->usb2_phy);
+	if (ret == -EBUSY)
+		ret = 0;
+	if (!ret && dwc3_readl(dwc, DWC3_GUID) != DWC3_APPLE_SLEEP_GUID) {
+		ret = dwc3_apple_core_reinit(appledwc);
+		if (!ret) {
+			appledwc->sleep_reinit = true;
+			dev_dbg(appledwc->dev,
+				"core lost its registers in the sleep: set up again, xHCI starts over\n");
+			return 1;
+		}
+	}
+	if (ret)
+		dev_err(appledwc->dev, "not resuming xHCI, the core didn't come back: %d\n", ret);
+
+	return ret;
+}
+
+static const struct dwc3_glue_ops dwc3_apple_glue_ops = {
+	.pre_host_resume = dwc3_apple_pre_host_resume,
+};
+
 /*
  * The T8132 host start qualified with SuperSpeed: the initial host role is
  * published before the core initializes and the CIO block is set up before
@@ -250,6 +373,7 @@ static int dwc3_apple_core_probe(struct dwc3_apple *appledwc, enum dwc3_apple_st
 	WARN_ON_ONCE(appledwc->state != DWC3_APPLE_PROBE_PENDING);
 
 	appledwc->dwc.dev = appledwc->dev;
+	appledwc->dwc.glue_ops = &dwc3_apple_glue_ops;
 	probe_data.dwc = &appledwc->dwc;
 	probe_data.res = appledwc->mmio_resource;
 	probe_data.ignore_clocks_and_resets = true;
@@ -493,6 +617,7 @@ static int dwc3_apple_core_start(struct dwc3_apple *appledwc, enum dwc3_apple_st
 	if (!of_device_is_compatible(appledwc->dev->of_node, "apple,t8132-dwc3") ||
 	    dwc3_apple_host_before_core_init(appledwc, state))
 		dwc3_apple_setup_cio(appledwc);
+	dwc3_apple_setup_hibernation(appledwc);
 	appledwc->core_active = true;
 	appledwc->usb2_power_off_pending = BIT(appledwc->dwc.num_usb2_ports) - 1;
 	appledwc->usb3_power_off_pending = BIT(appledwc->dwc.num_usb3_ports) - 1;
@@ -595,6 +720,152 @@ core_exit:
 	return ret;
 }
 
+static int dwc3_apple_exit(struct dwc3_apple *appledwc);
+
+/* A hub that hasn't enumerated by then didn't come back. */
+#define DWC3_APPLE_HUB_CHECK_MS 5000
+
+/* Any way a fixed-hub host came out of a sleep, its hub must be there a little later. */
+static void dwc3_apple_check_hub_later(struct dwc3_apple *appledwc)
+{
+	if (appledwc->fixed_hub)
+		mod_delayed_work(system_freezable_wq, &appledwc->hub_check,
+				 msecs_to_jiffies(DWC3_APPLE_HUB_CHECK_MS));
+}
+
+/*
+ * Bring a controller up again after a sleep that stopped it or lost it. A fixed-hub host
+ * starts as at probe, where the core has no role yet, so dwc3_core_soft_reset() runs too:
+ * rebinding the driver brought the J700's hub back where a restart without it didn't.
+ */
+static int dwc3_apple_start_after_sleep(struct dwc3_apple *appledwc, enum dwc3_apple_state state)
+{
+	int ret;
+
+	lockdep_assert_held(&appledwc->lock);
+	if (appledwc->fixed_hub)
+		appledwc->dwc.current_dr_role = 0;
+	ret = dwc3_apple_init(appledwc, state);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_err(appledwc->dev, "Failed to start after sleep, err=%d\n", ret);
+		return ret;
+	}
+	dwc3_apple_check_hub_later(appledwc);
+	return 0;
+}
+
+/* Whether the fixed hub is on its USB2 root port. Takes the xHCI device's lock. */
+static bool dwc3_apple_hub_present(struct dwc3_apple *appledwc)
+{
+	struct platform_device *xhci = appledwc->dwc.xhci;
+	struct usb_device *root;
+	struct usb_hcd *hcd;
+	bool present = true;
+
+	lockdep_assert_held(&appledwc->lock);
+	if (!xhci)
+		return true;
+
+	device_lock(&xhci->dev);
+	hcd = xhci->dev.driver ? platform_get_drvdata(xhci) : NULL;
+	root = hcd ? hcd->self.root_hub : NULL;
+	if (root) {
+		usb_lock_device(root);
+		present = usb_hub_find_child(root, appledwc->hub_port);
+		usb_unlock_device(root);
+	}
+	device_unlock(&xhci->dev);
+
+	return present;
+}
+
+/* Whether @udev or a device below it can wake the system. Takes the hubs' locks. */
+static bool dwc3_apple_can_wake(struct usb_device *udev)
+{
+	struct usb_device *child;
+	bool wake = device_may_wakeup(&udev->dev);
+	int port1;
+
+	if (wake || !udev->maxchild)
+		return wake;
+
+	usb_lock_device(udev);
+	usb_hub_for_each_child(udev, port1, child) {
+		wake = dwc3_apple_can_wake(child);
+		if (wake)
+			break;
+	}
+	usb_unlock_device(udev);
+
+	return wake;
+}
+
+/*
+ * The fixed hub's ports with a device at or below them that can wake the system, bit n for
+ * port n. The hub's own setting doesn't count. Takes the xHCI device's lock and the hubs'.
+ */
+static unsigned long dwc3_apple_hub_wake_ports(struct dwc3_apple *appledwc)
+{
+	struct platform_device *xhci = appledwc->dwc.xhci;
+	struct usb_device *root, *hub, *child;
+	unsigned long ports = 0;
+	struct usb_hcd *hcd;
+	int port1;
+
+	lockdep_assert_held(&appledwc->lock);
+	if (!xhci)
+		return 0;
+
+	device_lock(&xhci->dev);
+	hcd = xhci->dev.driver ? platform_get_drvdata(xhci) : NULL;
+	root = hcd ? hcd->self.root_hub : NULL;
+	if (root) {
+		usb_lock_device(root);
+		hub = usb_hub_find_child(root, appledwc->hub_port);
+		if (hub) {
+			usb_lock_device(hub);
+			usb_hub_for_each_child(hub, port1, child) {
+				if (port1 < BITS_PER_LONG && dwc3_apple_can_wake(child))
+					ports |= BIT(port1);
+			}
+			usb_unlock_device(hub);
+		}
+		usb_unlock_device(root);
+	}
+	device_unlock(&xhci->dev);
+
+	return ports;
+}
+
+/*
+ * Once after a sleep: if the hub isn't there, stop the host and start it again, with the
+ * hub's link down for a moment first, as unbinding and binding the driver does. This also
+ * catches an xHCI that didn't come back after the core was set up again.
+ */
+static void dwc3_apple_hub_check(struct work_struct *work)
+{
+	struct dwc3_apple *appledwc = container_of(to_delayed_work(work), struct dwc3_apple,
+						   hub_check);
+	int ret;
+
+	guard(mutex)(&appledwc->lock);
+	if (appledwc->shutting_down || appledwc->state != DWC3_APPLE_HOST ||
+	    dwc3_apple_hub_present(appledwc))
+		return;
+
+	dev_warn(appledwc->dev, "hub not back after the sleep: starting the host again\n");
+	ret = dwc3_apple_exit(appledwc);
+	if (ret) {
+		dev_err(appledwc->dev, "Failed to stop the host again, err=%d\n", ret);
+		return;
+	}
+	msleep(500);
+	appledwc->dwc.current_dr_role = 0;
+	ret = dwc3_apple_init(appledwc, DWC3_APPLE_HOST);
+	if (ret)
+		dev_err(appledwc->dev, "Failed to start the host again, err=%d\n", ret);
+}
+
 static int dwc3_apple_exit(struct dwc3_apple *appledwc)
 {
 	lockdep_assert_held(&appledwc->lock);
@@ -713,23 +984,88 @@ static int dwc3_apple_setup_role_switch(struct dwc3_apple *appledwc)
 	return 0;
 }
 
+/*
+ * After a sleep in place: the PHY says whether the core came back to D0, and the GUID register
+ * whether it kept its registers. A core set up again at the resume kept its host and devices,
+ * which reset-resumed. Otherwise restart the host as after a stop, and the devices enumerate
+ * again. With a core that may be unpowered even that would touch it, so USB stays off, and
+ * later sleeps are refused, as xHCI's suspend would touch it too. Returns whether the host
+ * was kept.
+ */
+static bool dwc3_apple_sleep_kept(struct dwc3_apple *appledwc)
+{
+	struct dwc3 *dwc = &appledwc->dwc;
+	u32 guid = 0;
+	int ret;
+
+	appledwc->sleep_in_place = false;
+	ret = apple_atcphy_sleep_result(appledwc->usb2_phy);
+	if (ret == -ENODEV) {
+		appledwc->sleep_dead = true;
+		dev_err(appledwc->dev, "the core didn't come back from the sleep: USB off until a reboot\n");
+		return false;
+	}
+	if (appledwc->sleep_reinit) {
+		appledwc->sleep_reinit = false;
+		dev_dbg(appledwc->dev, "host kept through the sleep, set up again\n");
+		dwc3_apple_check_hub_later(appledwc);
+		return true;
+	}
+	if (!ret || ret == -EBUSY) {
+		guid = dwc3_readl(dwc, DWC3_GUID);
+		if (guid == DWC3_APPLE_SLEEP_GUID) {
+			dwc3_writel(dwc, DWC3_GUID, appledwc->sleep_guid);
+			dev_dbg(appledwc->dev, "host kept through the sleep\n");
+			dwc3_apple_check_hub_later(appledwc);
+			return true;
+		}
+		ret = -EIO;
+	}
+
+	dev_warn(appledwc->dev, "host lost its state in the sleep (%d, GUID %#x): restarting it\n",
+		 ret, guid);
+	ret = dwc3_apple_exit(appledwc);
+	if (ret)
+		dev_err(appledwc->dev, "Failed to stop after sleep, err=%d\n", ret);
+	else
+		dwc3_apple_start_after_sleep(appledwc, DWC3_APPLE_HOST);
+
+	return false;
+}
+
 /*
  * Runs before the freezer, when the sleep's real target isn't known yet, so this goes by the
  * state "mem" means now. Writing "freeze" with deep selected costs a USB restart and nothing else.
  * A stop that fails refuses the sleep. PM_POST_SUSPEND follows an abandoned sleep as well.
  */
